{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779484515,"nonce":"0atUUa1GPt2cNUs57GQEfKH0/b4=","title":"Studio recurrent-edge UX and stateful-node inspector","message":"Parent: 0258fac\nRelated: 75a4665, 0866f1d, bd5aefb, 93f79f2\n\n## Summary\nAdd first-class Studio UX for recurrent/cycle edges and lightweight stateful-node inspection.\n\n## Decisions\n- A same-step port wire remains a solid ordinary edge.\n- When a new connection completes a directed cycle, the edge that completed the cycle should become a recurrent edge by default.\n- A recurrent edge means source value at step `t` feeds target value at step `t + 1`; it is not an instantaneous algebraic loop.\n- Recurrent edges should be visually distinct from instant wires, likely dashed or otherwise marked, and should expose their recurrent/init semantics in the edge properties panel.\n- Stateful nodes may get a small non-text visual cue, but node bodies should not print state metadata by default.\n- Selecting a stateful node should show a properties-panel section for its state slots/components, initializers, and relevant probe/intervention/value affordances.\n- Prefer explicit recurrent edges inside opened subgraphs over replacing them with always-visible state-slot projections.\n\n## UX Requirements\n- Creating `output -\u003e input` on the same node, or any edge that closes a cycle, automatically creates a recurrent edge rather than a same-step edge.\n- The default recurrent cut should be the newly dropped edge, not an arbitrary hidden DFS choice.\n- Users should be able to see which edge is the time cut in loops like `network -\u003e mechanics -\u003e feedback -\u003e network`.\n- Recurrent-edge properties should explain init source, shape/dtype compatibility, and whether the edge is executable.\n- Node properties should show state slots only when the selected component actually owns persistent state.\n\n## Out of Scope\n- Multirate or inner-rollout subgraph execution.\n- A full state browser for arbitrary PyTree selector authoring; that remains under selector/state-targeting issues.\n- Runtime mutation of Equinox module fields.\n\n## Done Criteria\n- Studio schema/types can represent recurrent edges distinctly from instant wires.\n- Canvas creation and rendering make recurrent edges obvious and stable.\n- Validation treats same-step cycles as invalid unless at least one cycle-closing edge is recurrent.\n- Network internals can show GRU/LSTM hidden recurrence explicitly when opened.\n- Properties panel exposes recurrent-edge init/status and selected-node state-slot summaries without cluttering the canvas.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}